Subject: rtl81x9 on alpha
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=103893014201038

Hello

I've been working to port the rtl81x9 driver from NetBSD, and without
bigger problems i think it..
(callout_* replaced by timeout_*, struct ifnet differences etc..)

The reason to do the port was that the current driver has bug that crashes
the kernel on alpha (unaligned memory access):

--- sys/dev/ic/rtl81x9.c.orig	Tue Jul  2 22:38:55 2002
+++ sys/dev/ic/rtl81x9.c	Tue Oct  8 16:13:14 2002
@@ -1177,7 +1177,8 @@
 		if (sc->rl_cdata.rl_tx_chain[i] != NULL) {
 			m_freem(sc->rl_cdata.rl_tx_chain[i]);
 			sc->rl_cdata.rl_tx_chain[i] = NULL;
-			CSR_WRITE_4(sc, RL_TXADDR0 + i, 0x00000000);
+			CSR_WRITE_4(sc,
+			    RL_TXADDR0 + (i * sizeof(u_int32_t)), 0x00000000);
 		}
 	}

However, fixing the above doesn't remedy the problem that the
interface seems to be "collecting" corrupted packets (verified by a
constantly increasing number of Ierrs in netstat -I rl0) and generally
doesn't seem to work.

Back to the NetBSD driver: It seems to suffer from the same problems, so
not much help from there - except for some experience in porting *BSD
drivers, of course..

Today i installed NetBSD 1.6 on the same Alphastation 200 and here the
thing works OK. Some time ago i tested it successfully on an Alphaserver
1000 running FreeBSD (4.6-RELEASE or 5-CURRENT; can't remember now..)

The card in question is an Accton MPX 5030/5038 PCI.

Do you have any ideas why this doesn't work on OpenBSD/alpha ?


Greetings,
Dennis Lindroos

